// Original teaching material. Shared dossiers are intentionally revisited on revision days.
const D = (title, sections, rows, terms, timeline, trap, question, answer) => ({title,sections:sections.map(([heading,text])=>({heading,text})),comparison:{headers:['Concept / example','What to remember','Why it matters'],rows},terms:terms.map(([term,meaning])=>({term,meaning})),timeline:timeline.map(([date,event])=>({date,event})),trap,practice:{question,answer}});
export const dossiers = {
  sources:D('How to read historical evidence',[
    ['From an object to an argument','Begin with context. A coin recovered in an undisturbed archaeological layer can help date that layer, but a coin purchased without a known findspot tells us much less about its original use. Ask what was found, where it was found and how securely it is dated. The same discipline applies to texts: a surviving manuscript may be much later than the work it transmits.'],
    ['Corroboration and silence','Different sources can support one another without saying exactly the same thing. A donation inscription may name a merchant; a workshop and imported objects may show the economy within which that merchant operated. Silence is more difficult: the absence of a surviving text does not prove that a practice never existed. Preservation favours some materials and institutions over others.'],
    ['Use geography as an explanation','A river can support irrigation and transport while also creating flood risks. A mountain pass can channel migration rather than prevent it. Historical explanations should link an environmental feature to a mechanism and a dated example. For Magadha, combine river routes and productive land with political choices. Avoid explaining every successful kingdom simply by calling its location favourable.']
  ],[['Inscription','Text on a durable surface','Can record grants, orders or praise; its purpose still needs scrutiny.'],['Numismatics','Study of coins','Connects authority, symbols, circulation and chronology.'],['Archaeology','Material remains in context','Reconstructs lives that elite texts may overlook.'],['Chronicle','Narrative of events','Author, patron and intended audience shape selection.']], [['Epigraphy','The study of inscriptions.'],['Stratigraphy','The relationship between archaeological layers.'],['Corroboration','Testing a claim against independent evidence.']], [['Before writing','Material evidence is especially important.'],['Early historic period','Inscriptions and coins widen the evidence base.'],['Medieval centuries','Chronicles and regional texts provide further perspectives.']], 'A primary source is close to its subject; it is not automatically unbiased.', 'How would you investigate a king’s claim to have conquered a distant region?', 'Compare the claim with local inscriptions, administrative records, coin distributions and settlement evidence. Distinguish a raid, tribute relationship and lasting administration instead of treating them as the same form of conquest.'),
  farming:D('From mobile livelihoods to settled production',[
    ['An uneven transition','Foraging required detailed knowledge of seasons, animals and plants. It was not simply an unsuccessful version of farming. Food production developed through experimentation and changing relationships with particular species. Some communities farmed while continuing to hunt; others specialised in herding or exchanged forest products with cultivators. Archaeological periods describe patterns, not a universal timetable followed by every community.'],
    ['What changed in daily life','Storage allowed food to be kept beyond the harvest, but also required protection and organisation. Repeated settlement encouraged investment in houses and facilities. Farming could increase output while bringing risks from crop failure and a narrower diet. Changes in labour, property and exchange must therefore be investigated rather than assumed to be uniformly beneficial.'],
    ['Reading the archaeological record','Plant remains, animal bones, tools and habitation structures answer different questions. Domesticated species may be identified through physical changes, while sickles or grinding stones point to activities rather than one guaranteed economic system. Mehrgarh is a major example for early farming in the north-west. Megalithic burials provide a different kind of evidence, especially for communities in peninsular India.']
  ],[['Palaeolithic','Predominantly hunting and gathering','Study tools alongside habitats and mobility.'],['Mesolithic','Microliths are a common marker','Regional livelihoods remained diverse.'],['Neolithic','Food production and polished tools in many settings','Different traits did not always appear together.'],['Chalcolithic','Copper and stone used together','Metal did not immediately replace stone.']], [['Domestication','Changes in species through sustained human selection and management.'],['Microlith','A small worked stone implement.'],['Surplus','Production beyond immediate consumption needs.']], [['Long prehistoric span','Foraging economies adapted to varied environments.'],['Early village life','Farming, herding and storage developed regionally.'],['Later settlements','Production and exchange supported greater specialisation.']], 'Permanent settlement and farming are related, but neither automatically proves the other.', 'Why is “farming was immediate progress for everyone” an inadequate argument?', 'It ignores regional choices, continuing mixed livelihoods, crop risks, labour demands and possible inequalities. Explain both increased production and the new vulnerabilities associated with dependence on harvests.'),
  harappa:D('Harappan cities: economy, society and evidence',[
    ['Urban systems','Harappan urbanism rested on relationships between cities, smaller settlements, agricultural zones and sources of raw material. Roads, drains and wells reveal planning, but the most useful question is how these facilities were maintained. Standardised practices suggest coordination; they do not by themselves identify whether authority belonged to a king, councils or some other institution. Compare settlements before generalising from one famous city.'],
    ['Crafts and exchange','Bead production required raw material, specialised techniques and labour. Shell objects, metals and stone linked distant resource zones. Standard weights and seals are relevant to exchange and administration, although their precise uses are debated. Contact with Mesopotamia places Harappan communities within wider networks. A foreign object can indicate contact without proving that its maker lived at the site where it was found.'],
    ['Change after the urban phase','The decline of large urban centres should be separated from the disappearance of every associated practice. Settlement shifted and regional cultures developed. Environmental change, river behaviour and economic reorganisation are among the issues historians investigate. A strong answer recognises regional variation and the limitations of evidence instead of presenting one dramatic event as a complete explanation.']
  ],[['Mohenjo-daro','Great Bath and urban facilities','Use specific features as evidence rather than guessing their full social meaning.'],['Dholavira','Reservoirs and distinctive city divisions','Shows adaptation to local water conditions.'],['Harappa','Major excavated urban centre','Gives its name to the civilisation.'],['Rakhigarhi','Large settlement in present-day Haryana','Reminds us that the civilisation extended beyond the Indus river itself.']], [['Citadel','A conventionally named raised or fortified area at some sites.'],['Seal','An inscribed or decorated object used to make an impression.'],['Urbanisation','Growth of settlements with concentrated population and specialised functions.']], [['c. 2600 BCE','Beginning of the Mature Harappan phase.'],['c. 2600–1900 BCE','Major urban settlements and extensive exchange.'],['After c. 1900 BCE','Regional changes and decline of many large cities.']], 'An undeciphered script cannot be used to make a secure translation of Harappan religious or political titles.', 'How do standard weights and different city layouts fit together?', 'Standard weights indicate shared practices across networks, while varied layouts show local choices and environments. Common cultural features do not require identical institutions or urban design everywhere.'),
  vedic:D('Vedic society, kinship and social change',[
    ['Texts and chronology','The Vedas are collections transmitted over generations. Historians distinguish broad early and later contexts, while recognising that individual passages are difficult to date precisely. The Rigveda contains hymns, not a comprehensive description of all inhabitants of the subcontinent. Archaeological cultures and textual communities should not be equated without additional evidence.'],
    ['Economy and political organisation','Cattle were central to wealth and ritual vocabulary in early Vedic texts. Over time, agriculture and more territorially defined political units became increasingly prominent. Assemblies, chiefs and kings must be understood within changing institutions. Expansion towards the Ganga plains involved interaction with existing communities; it was not settlement of an empty landscape.'],
    ['Norms and actual relationships','Later texts elaborated social classification, ritual obligations and kinship rules. Patriliny concerned descent, while rules of marriage and gotra sought to structure social relationships. Prescriptions about women or social groups do not establish that all individuals lived identically. Use narratives and inscriptions to examine negotiations, exceptions and unequal access to property or authority.']
  ],[['Early Vedic emphasis','Cattle, kin groups and hymnic ritual','Do not erase evidence of cultivation.'],['Later Vedic development','Agrarian expansion and territorial power','A broad trend, not a single date of transformation.'],['Varna','Normative classification','Not identical to every historically existing jati.'],['Patriliny','Descent through the male line','Must be distinguished from rules of residence and property.']], [['Gotra','A lineage category important in marriage prescriptions.'],['Jana','A term associated with a people or community.'],['Janapada','A territorial domain associated with a political community.']], [['Early Vedic context','North-western river landscapes figure prominently.'],['Later Vedic context','Ganga–Yamuna regions become increasingly significant.'],['c. sixth century BCE','Larger territorial states form part of a new political setting.']], 'A rule in a normative text is evidence of an ideal, not proof of universal compliance.', 'Write a two-part comparison of early and later Vedic society.', 'First compare economy and political organisation: cattle-centred wealth and kin groups versus greater agrarian and territorial emphasis. Then discuss social differentiation and ritual elaboration, qualifying the comparison with regional variation and the limits of the sources.'),
  magadha:D('Mahajanapadas and the rise of Magadha',[
    ['Territorial power','Larger states required resources beyond a chief’s immediate following. Agricultural production, taxation, fortified centres and armed forces reinforced one another. Monarchies coexisted with gana-sanghas, in which a restricted body of leading members exercised authority. This variation matters: early political history is not a story of only one institutional form.'],
    ['The Magadhan advantage','River connections supported movement of goods and people. Productive land and access to forests and mineral-bearing regions offered resources, but resources were useful only when organised. Bimbisara, Ajatashatru and subsequent rulers employed alliances, conquest and institutional consolidation. A geographical explanation becomes stronger when connected to these concrete political actions.'],
    ['Bihar as a connected landscape','Rajgir’s setting and Pataliputra’s river connections illustrate different strategic advantages. Vaishali and Buddhist and Jain sites show that political centres existed alongside religious networks. Nalanda belongs to a later institutional history and should not be inserted into every earlier period. A Bihar answer gains precision when it separates site, date, institution and historical process.']
  ],[['Monarchy','Authority centred on a ruler','Dynasty, taxation and military organisation need explanation.'],['Gana-sangha','Collective authority among a limited group','Not universal adult democracy.'],['Rajgir','An early Magadhan centre','Topography and fortification mattered.'],['Pataliputra','Strategic location within river networks','Communication supported wider political integration.']], [['Mahajanapada','A major territorial polity of the early historic period.'],['Tribute','A payment expressing a political relationship.'],['State formation','Development of institutions that organise authority and resources.']], [['c. sixth century BCE','Mahajanapadas and new religious movements.'],['Magadhan expansion','Alliances and conquests strengthen regional power.'],['Fourth century BCE','Nanda and then Mauryan power widen political integration.']], 'Natural advantages help explain possibilities; they do not make the success of a kingdom inevitable.', 'Explain Magadha’s rise using three linked mechanisms.', 'Agricultural surplus supported revenue; river routes supported movement and communication; rulers converted resources into military and political control through alliances, conquest and administration.'),
  religion:D('Buddhism, Jainism and religious patronage',[
    ['New questions in a changing society','Urban growth and political change formed the setting for debates about ritual, suffering, conduct and liberation. Buddhist and Jain traditions were part of a wider intellectual field. Treating them only as protests against one social practice overlooks their philosophical and institutional development. Their teachings also evolved through debate and the formation of different schools.'],
    ['Ideas and institutions','The Buddhist path addresses suffering and its cessation through ethical and mental discipline. Jain teaching emphasises liberation of the soul from karmic bondage through restraint and knowledge. Both traditions developed monastic communities and relationships with lay supporters. Shared ethical concerns, such as non-violence, do not erase major differences about the self, practice or liberation.'],
    ['Monuments as social evidence','A stupa could preserve relics and support devotion and commemoration. Donative inscriptions reveal participation by people other than rulers. Trade routes, towns and religious institutions could reinforce one another, but patronage had multiple motives. When studying a monument, ask who funded it, when it was enlarged, which community used it and how its imagery communicated meaning.']
  ],[['Buddhism','No permanent, unchanging self','Do not substitute the Jain doctrine of an enduring soul.'],['Jainism','Liberation of the soul from karmic bondage','Restraint and non-violence are central.'],['Sangha','A religious community, especially monastic in this context','Institutional organisation helped transmit teaching.'],['Stupa','Relic, commemorative or devotional structure','Architecture can be evidence for patronage and ritual.']], [['Dukkha','Suffering or unsatisfactoriness in Buddhist teaching.'],['Tirthankara','A revered teacher in the Jain tradition.'],['Circumambulation','Movement around a sacred object or structure as a devotional act.']], [['c. sixth–fifth centuries BCE','Broad setting of the Buddha and Mahavira.'],['Mauryan period','Ashoka becomes a prominent Buddhist patron.'],['Later centuries','Monastic networks and monuments expand and change.']], 'Mahavira is the twenty-fourth Tirthankara in Jain tradition, not the first.', 'Why is a religious monument useful for social history?', 'Its inscriptions, architecture and location can reveal donors, crafts, networks and ritual practices. Explain what survives while acknowledging that unnamed worshippers and workers remain harder to recover.'),
  empires:D('Mauryan government and Ashoka’s dhamma',[
    ['An empire of different regions','Imperial control could involve provincial centres, routes and strategic settlements rather than an equally dense bureaucracy everywhere. Sources indicate ambitions of supervision, but actual governing arrangements depended on distance and local conditions. Distinguish the territory claimed by a ruler from uniform direct administration across every part of it.'],
    ['Read each source on its own terms','Megasthenes wrote as an outsider; his account survives through later writers. The Arthashastra is a prescriptive work with a complex textual history. Ashokan inscriptions communicate royal orders and ethical messages. These sources can be compared, but they should not be combined as though written at the same time for the same purpose.'],
    ['Ethics and communication','Ashoka’s public response to Kalinga expresses remorse and a preference for dhamma. Messages about restraint, welfare and respect among communities also helped communicate across a diverse empire. Dhamma cannot be reduced either to compulsory Buddhism or to the abolition of state power. The inscriptions themselves are evidence of how royal authority sought to address its subjects.']
  ],[['Chandragupta Maurya','Foundation of Mauryan power','Connect political expansion with Magadha.'],['Megasthenes','An external observer','Transmission and viewpoint affect the evidence.'],['Major Rock Edict XIII','Kalinga and the language of remorse','Useful for analysing public royal claims.'],['Dhamma','Broad ethical conduct','Not a replacement for every administrative institution.']], [['Prakrit','A group of Middle Indo-Aryan languages used in many inscriptions.'],['Brahmi','A script used in many Ashokan inscriptions.'],['Edict','An authoritative public proclamation.']], [['Late fourth century BCE','Mauryan power established.'],['Third century BCE','Ashoka’s reign and inscriptions.'],['After Kalinga','Dhamma becomes central to his public message.']], 'Ashoka’s religious commitment and the intended audience of his ethical messages are related but distinct questions.', 'How can an ethical policy also serve political communication?', 'A ruler can address shared standards of conduct across different communities while presenting himself as concerned with welfare and order. This political function does not mean that the ethical commitment was necessarily insincere.'),
  classical:D('Trade, regional states and cultural production',[
    ['Beyond the idea of decline','The period after the Mauryas included regional kingdoms, transregional exchange and major artistic developments. Political plurality did not automatically stop commerce. Kushana and Satavahana histories connect inland routes, towns and religious institutions. In early Tamil regions, poems and archaeology illuminate exchange without providing a complete statistical account of society.'],
    ['Guptas and regional authority','Inscriptions and coins are essential for Gupta history. The Prayaga prashasti celebrates Samudragupta and describes different relationships with other rulers. Conquest, tribute and recognition were not the same. Land grants reveal transfers of rights and claims over revenue, but their effects must be studied regionally. Labels such as golden age should be tested against whose experience is being described.'],
    ['Art and learning in context','Gandhara and Mathura show different materials and artistic conventions within connected worlds. Ajanta’s caves and paintings developed under changing patronage; Nalanda became an important centre of learning. Literature and monuments survived through institutions, resources and skilled labour. An answer on cultural achievement becomes analytical when it explains those conditions and recognises social inequality.']
  ],[['Gandhara','North-western artistic traditions','Contact and adaptation matter more than a simple imported/local binary.'],['Mathura','A major centre of sculpture','Compare material, iconography and patronage.'],['Sangam ports','Korkai, Poompuhar and Muchiri','Connect literary evidence with maritime exchange.'],['Prashasti','Royal eulogy','Political claims require critical reading.']], [['Guild','An association linked to an occupation or craft.'],['Land grant','Transfer or recognition of rights, often recorded in inscriptions.'],['Patronage','Support that makes cultural or institutional activity possible.']], [['Post-Mauryan centuries','Regional powers and wider trade networks.'],['Fourth–sixth centuries CE','Major phase of Gupta rule.'],['Seventh century CE','Harsha and powerful regional kingdoms.']], 'A cultural achievement does not demonstrate prosperity or equality for every social group.', 'How would you qualify the description of the Gupta age as a golden age?', 'Identify specific achievements in art, literature or knowledge, then discuss their uneven social and regional distribution. Use the label as a claim to evaluate, not a substitute for evidence.'),
  regional:D('Regional kingdoms, temples and political networks',[
    ['Power through relationships','Regional rulers obtained resources through farming, tribute, grants and military action. Authority depended on intermediaries as well as royal orders. Terms such as samanta describe relationships that changed over time. A subordinate could become powerful, compete with a ruler or establish an independent lineage; political status was not permanently fixed.'],
    ['Temples and local institutions','Temples could employ workers, receive grants and participate in production and redistribution. Chola inscriptions illuminate assemblies and qualifications for participation. These institutions show local organisation, but restricted eligibility makes comparison with modern democracy misleading. Separate village institutions from an assumption that all residents possessed equal political rights.'],
    ['The eighteenth-century comparison','Later regional powers such as Bengal, Awadh and Hyderabad combined autonomy with elements of Mughal legitimacy. Their growth demonstrates political reorganisation rather than a universal vacuum. Compare different regional periods carefully: similar words like local power do not make Chola and eighteenth-century institutions identical. Specify the date and mechanism in every comparison.']
  ],[['Cholas','Royal, local and temple institutions','Study how resources connected these levels.'],['Palas / Pratiharas / Rashtrakutas','Interregional competition','Kannauj is important in the political narrative.'],['Bengal / Awadh / Hyderabad','Autonomous eighteenth-century powers','Mughal decline did not end all administration.'],['Maratha expansion','Military and revenue networks','Connect regional ambitions with wider political change.']], [['Brahmadeya','A land grant associated with Brahman recipients.'],['Sabha','An assembly associated in this context with certain Brahman settlements.'],['Legitimacy','The grounds on which authority is justified or recognised.']], [['Early medieval centuries','Regional lineages and new political relationships.'],['Chola expansion','Temples and local institutions leave rich inscriptional evidence.'],['Eighteenth century','Mughal successor states and other powers expand.']], 'Local government in the past does not imply universal political equality.', 'Why should temples appear in an answer about political economy?', 'They could receive resources, employ artisans and workers, organise ritual prestige and interact with local authorities. Explain these roles without assuming every temple had the same scale or function.'),
  sultanate:D('Delhi Sultanate: institutions and technological change',[
    ['Conquest is not administration','Military success created opportunities but did not guarantee stable revenue or obedience. The Sultanate had to manage communications, local resistance, powerful nobles and pressure from outside its frontiers. A dynasty list gives chronology; the deeper question is how each ruler tried to translate armed strength into durable control.'],
    ['Revenue and service','Iqta assignments connected revenue collection with obligations to the state. Holders were expected to maintain troops and meet other demands, while rulers sought supervision and accounting. Do not describe an iqta as an unrestricted hereditary estate. Khalji and Tughlaq policies should be compared through their aims, implementation problems and effects, rather than memorised as isolated experiments.'],
    ['Technology and society','Paper making, textile techniques and building practices spread through networks of artisans, merchants and patrons. True arches and domes involved structural knowledge and adaptation. Wider use can be historically important even when a technique was not invented in that period. For Mains, connect technology to labour, production, record keeping and urban life, and acknowledge uneven diffusion across regions.']
  ],[['Mamluks','Early consolidation at Delhi','Place rulers before comparing later policies.'],['Alauddin Khalji','Market and military measures','Connect regulation with the needs of a large army.'],['Muhammad bin Tughlaq','Ambitious fiscal and administrative experiments','Separate objectives from outcomes.'],['Iqta','Revenue assignment linked to obligations','Not equivalent to absolute land ownership.']], [['Muqti','A holder or administrator of an iqta in Sultanate usage.'],['Arcuate','Construction using arches and related vaulting.'],['Trabeate','Construction based on posts and horizontal beams.']], [['1206','Conventional beginning of the Delhi Sultanate.'],['1290–1320','Khalji dynasty.'],['1320–1414','Tughlaq dynasty.'],['1526','First Battle of Panipat ends Lodi rule.']], 'Introduction, adoption, diffusion and invention are different claims about a technology.', 'How should an answer connect paper making with social change?', 'Discuss more accessible written communication, record keeping and learned activity, while noting that literacy and access remained unequal and that political patronage and markets affected production.'),
  mughal:D('Mughal power and the agrarian system',[
    ['Ranks and revenue','The mansab system organised imperial service through ranks, with zat and sawar expressing distinct aspects of status and cavalry obligations. Jagir assignments supported remuneration through claims on revenue. Confusing mansab and jagir loses the distinction between a person’s rank and the fiscal arrangement supporting service. Transfers and supervision were important to the system’s operation.'],
    ['Village society was not uniform','Peasants differed in holdings, resources and bargaining power. Zamindars could possess local rights and influence that predated a particular imperial appointment. The state depended on intermediaries while also trying to regulate them. Revenue assessment describes a claim, whereas actual collection depended on harvests, resistance, negotiation and administrative capacity.'],
    ['Sources and the Sur interlude','The Ain-i Akbari offers a systematic imperial view but should be checked against regional evidence. Sher Shah’s earlier administration belongs to a separate dynasty within the sixteenth-century chronology. Roads, sarais and revenue measures illustrate the relationship between communication and rule. Do not collapse all administrative developments into a timeless Mughal system.']
  ],[['Mansab','Rank in imperial service','Status and obligations.'],['Jagir','Revenue assignment','A means of supporting remuneration and service.'],['Zamindar','Holder of varied local rights and influence','Not simply a synonym for every revenue official.'],['Assessment / collection','Demand calculated / payment realised','A difference between administrative design and practice.']], [['Zat','A component of mansab associated with personal rank.'],['Sawar','A component associated with cavalry obligations.'],['Ain-i Akbari','Abul Fazl’s account of imperial administration and resources.']], [['1526','Babur’s victory at Panipat.'],['1540–1555','Sur rule interrupts Humayun’s reign.'],['1556–1605','Akbar’s reign.'],['Eighteenth century','Regional power grows amid weakening central authority.']], 'A jagir is not the same as a mansab, and neither automatically means private ownership of every field.', 'Explain why the amount assessed might differ from revenue collected.', 'Harvest variation, local bargaining, resistance, intermediary power and uneven enforcement could prevent the state from realising its formal demand.'),
  vijayanagara:D('Vijayanagara: landscape, water and authority',[
    ['A capital within its environment','Rocky hills, the Tungabhadra and cultivable areas shaped the capital’s development. Fortifications worked with the terrain, while reservoirs and canals addressed water supply. Studying the city as a system links its famous monuments to farms, routes and labour. This is more revealing than a catalogue of buildings separated from their setting.'],
    ['Reading different urban spaces','The sacred centre, royal centre and areas of settlement had different but related functions. Ceremonies communicated power; religious institutions attracted people and resources; markets connected production with consumption. Archaeological names for structures may be modern interpretations. A label should not be treated as a fully documented account of a building’s original use.'],
    ['Accounts and political change','Travellers described wealth, ceremonies and urban organisation through their own interests. Compare these accounts with ruins, inscriptions and the landscape. The defeat at Talikota in 1565 profoundly affected the capital, but the wider political history continued beyond one battle. Likewise, attribute particular waterworks to the correct ruler rather than assuming all achievements belong to Krishnadevaraya.']
  ],[['Tungabhadra','River and water source','Connect environment to agricultural and urban supply.'],['Devaraya I','Associated with major waterworks','A useful ruler–infrastructure association.'],['Royal centre','Political and ceremonial spaces','Interpret structures using multiple kinds of evidence.'],['Sacred centre','Religious sites and institutions','Religion, economy and authority could overlap.']], [['Aqueduct','A structure or channel carrying water across a landscape.'],['Fortification','Defensive works around a place or route.'],['Urban hinterland','The surrounding area supplying and interacting with a town.']], [['Fourteenth century','Rise of Vijayanagara.'],['Early fifteenth century','Devaraya I’s rule.'],['1509–1529','Krishnadevaraya’s reign.'],['1565','Battle of Talikota and its aftermath.']], 'The destruction or decline of a capital is not automatically the immediate end of every associated political institution.', 'Explain water management as both an economic and political activity.', 'Waterworks support cultivation and urban supply, while their construction displays authority’s ability to mobilise resources and labour. Use the capital’s environment and a named example to connect the two.'),
  culture:D('Devotion, architecture and regional cultures',[
    ['Diversity within devotional traditions','Bhakti included different ideas of the divine, forms of worship and social relationships. Alvars and Nayanars belong to southern devotional traditions; Kabir’s compositions raise different questions about religious identity and authority. Use saguna and nirguna as analytical distinctions, while recognising that individual traditions may resist simple classification.'],
    ['Institutions and transmission','Sufi teaching developed through lineages and communities associated with teachers. Khanqahs could provide instruction, hospitality and spaces of devotion. Relations with rulers differed across time and silsilas. Hagiographies often communicate ideals and miracles; they should be read differently from administrative accounts rather than treated as literal transcripts of every event.'],
    ['Language and artistic exchange','Regional literature, music and architecture developed through patrons and mobile specialists. Buildings can combine techniques and forms drawn from different traditions. Political rivalry did not prevent artistic exchange, and cultural interaction did not eliminate conflict or social hierarchy. Explain a concrete mechanism—translation, artisan movement or a devotional network—before making a broad claim about synthesis.']
  ],[['Saguna devotion','Divine attributes and forms','A conceptual distinction within varied practices.'],['Nirguna devotion','Emphasis beyond fixed attributes','Do not assume every teacher expressed it identically.'],['Silsila','A Sufi spiritual lineage','Shows transmission and institutional affiliation.'],['Monument','Patronage embodied in space','Date additions and identify artisans where possible.']], [['Khanqah','A place associated with Sufi teaching and community life.'],['Hagiography','A life of a holy person written with devotional purposes.'],['Vernacular','A language used by a local or regional community.']], [['Early medieval south','Alvar and Nayanar traditions develop.'],['Later medieval centuries','Regional devotional and Sufi networks expand.'],['Mughal and regional courts','Diverse forms of artistic patronage continue.']], 'Social criticism in a poem does not prove that social inequality disappeared among all its followers.', 'Give a balanced conclusion about devotional traditions and social change.', 'They created languages and spaces for questioning authority and expressing devotion, but their social effects differed by region and community. Recognise both challenges to hierarchy and its continued presence.'),
  travellers:D('Travellers, towns and changing communities',[
    ['An observer has a position','Al-Biruni’s engagement with Sanskrit learning, Ibn Battuta’s mobile career and Bernier’s comparisons with Europe created different accounts. Their interests determined what they noticed. A claim about all Indian society may rest on a narrow set of encounters. Ask where an observer travelled, which languages he knew and whom he could meet.'],
    ['Towns as networks','A town might be a port, administrative centre, pilgrimage site or craft hub, often combining several roles. Artisans required raw materials and customers; merchants needed routes, credit and political arrangements. Urban history therefore links production, exchange and institutions. A traveller’s account of a spectacular market should be checked against other evidence rather than converted directly into a measure of general prosperity.'],
    ['Beyond settled-versus-tribal binaries','Pastoral and forest communities interacted with states through trade, tribute, conflict and alliance. Gond and Ahom histories show different processes of state building and social change. These groups were not frozen outside history. Changes in land use and political demand could reshape occupations, settlement and identities, while communities also influenced the states around them.']
  ],[['Al-Biruni','Eleventh-century intellectual observer','Compare learning and cultural translation.'],['Ibn Battuta','Fourteenth-century traveller','Useful for movement, cities and communication.'],['Bernier','Seventeenth-century European visitor','Comparisons may project European expectations.'],['Gonds / Ahoms','Different regional histories','Avoid one generic model for all communities.']], [['Itinerary','A route or sequence of travel.'],['Pastoralism','Livelihoods substantially based on herding.'],['Ethnocentrism','Interpreting others primarily through one’s own cultural assumptions.']], [['Eleventh century','Al-Biruni’s work on India.'],['Fourteenth century','Ibn Battuta’s travels.'],['Seventeenth century','Bernier’s observations of Mughal India.']], 'An eyewitness can be accurate about an event while drawing an unreliable generalisation from it.', 'How would you evaluate a traveller’s statement about landownership?', 'Define the rights he actually observed, compare local and administrative sources, and distinguish ownership, revenue claims and cultivation. Check whether European legal categories have distorted the description.'),
  company:D('From trading company to territorial government',[
    ['Commercial power needed political arrangements','European companies began with trade but relied on privileges, fortified settlements and armed competition. They operated within existing Indian markets and political systems. Their later territorial strength should not be projected back as an inevitable outcome of the first factory. Local alliances, finance and conflicts changed what the Company could achieve.'],
    ['Bengal and the revenue–army connection','Plassey and Buxar were different events with different political consequences. Victory and negotiation enabled the Company to obtain the Diwani in 1765. Revenue supported military expenditure; military power could secure new revenue. This feedback helps explain expansion, but it depended on Indian intermediaries, banking networks and particular decisions as well as European organisation.'],
    ['Expansion and parliamentary supervision','Subsidiary alliances imposed military and diplomatic constraints on allied rulers. Annexation could occur through different mechanisms, including the Doctrine of Lapse. Meanwhile, Parliament increasingly regulated the Company. The creation of the Governor-General of Bengal and the later designation of Governor-General of India belong to distinct legal stages. Separate territory, title and effective control.']
  ],[['Plassey, 1757','Victory closely tied to political intrigue','Do not confuse it with Buxar’s coalition and consequences.'],['Buxar, 1764','Military victory strengthens Company leverage','Important for the ensuing settlement and Bihar.'],['Diwani, 1765','Revenue rights in Bengal, Bihar and Orissa','Finance becomes central to political expansion.'],['Subsidiary alliance','Obligations under an unequal alliance','Not identical to annexation by lapse.']], [['Factory','A trading establishment in early Company usage.'],['Diwani','Revenue-administration rights in this context.'],['Annexation','Incorporation of territory into a ruler’s domain.']], [['1757','Plassey.'],['1764','Buxar.'],['1765','Diwani grant.'],['1833','Governor-General of India designation.']], 'Company commercial presence, territorial sovereignty and parliamentary control developed at different times.', 'Explain Company expansion without attributing it to military technology alone.', 'Combine revenue resources, credit, diplomacy, alliances, local conflicts and military organisation. Use Bengal to show how these factors reinforced one another rather than presenting a single-cause explanation.'),
  rural:D('Land settlements, the colonial economy and resistance',[
    ['Rights and demands','Revenue systems reorganised relationships among the state, intermediaries and cultivators. Under the Permanent Settlement, the state’s demand on zamindars was fixed; this did not fix every tenant’s rent or remove insecurity. Ryotwari and Mahalwari arrangements used different units and intermediaries. Variations in assessment, enforcement and recognised rights were as important as the names of the systems.'],
    ['Markets could create vulnerability','Commercial cultivation connected farmers to wider prices and credit. Debt, revenue deadlines and crop failure could interact. A crop might be profitable for some participants while exposing others to risk or coercion. Colonial economic criticism connected these conditions to trade policy, remittances and the drain of wealth. Deindustrialisation should be studied as uneven changes in crafts and employment, not the disappearance of every artisan.'],
    ['Resistance has a local history','Peasant, tribal and civil resistance arose from different combinations of grievances. Santhal and Kol movements require attention to land, debt, intermediaries and the historical geography of regions now including Jharkhand. Their aims should be reconstructed before calling them identical expressions of modern nationalism. Local struggles nonetheless illuminate the contested character of colonial rule.']
  ],[['Permanent Settlement','State demand fixed on zamindars','Tenant conditions could still change.'],['Ryotwari','Direct assessment of cultivators in its basic design','No automatic protection from high demands or crop failure.'],['Mahalwari','Assessment linked to village estates or groups','Implementation varied across regions.'],['Drain critique','Resources transferred without equivalent return','Connect economic argument with emerging nationalism.']], [['Rent','Payment by a cultivator to a holder of superior land rights.'],['Revenue','The state’s fiscal demand or receipts.'],['Commercialisation','Increasing production for markets rather than only direct consumption.']], [['1793','Permanent Settlement introduced in Bengal.'],['1831–32','Kol uprising.'],['1855–56','Santhal rebellion.'],['Later nineteenth century','Nationalist economic criticism develops.']], 'Fixed state revenue and fixed peasant rent are not the same thing.', 'Why could rising agricultural exports coexist with rural distress?', 'Higher exports do not reveal how benefits and risks were distributed. Revenue pressure, debt, unequal contracts, price changes and reduced security could burden cultivators even as trade expanded.'),
  revolt:D('1857: causes, participation and consequences',[
    ['Build a layered explanation','Military grievances included service conditions and fears about interference with religion. Political grievances involved annexation and the loss of authority; rural discontent reflected wider social and economic pressures. The cartridge controversy was a trigger within this setting. Explaining only the trigger cannot account for participation beyond soldiers or for differences between regions.'],
    ['Unity and diversity','Rebels sometimes invoked common symbols and sought wider legitimacy, but leaders and participants did not share one fully developed programme. Some defended dynastic claims; others pursued local concerns. Important centres included Delhi, Awadh, Kanpur, Jhansi and parts of Bihar. Regions that remained quieter are also part of the explanation and caution against unqualified claims of universal participation.'],
    ['What changed after suppression','Crown rule replaced Company government in 1858. Army organisation, relations with princes and official policy statements changed. The experience influenced later colonial attitudes and Indian political memory. Debates over whether to call it a mutiny, revolt or war of independence should be grounded in evidence about participation and goals, rather than resolved by a label alone.']
  ],[['Military grievances','Conditions, service and religious fears','Explain the initial military setting.'],['Political grievances','Annexation and dispossession','Connect affected rulers and elites.'],['Popular participation','Varied local demands','Shows why “only a barracks event” is insufficient.'],['Kunwar Singh','Important leader in Bihar','Use a regional example within the wider argument.']], [['Trigger','An immediate event that helps set a crisis in motion.'],['Structural cause','A deeper condition making a crisis possible.'],['Proclamation','An official public statement of policy or authority.']], [['May 1857','Rebellion at Meerut and movement towards Delhi.'],['1857–58','Major centres of rebellion and suppression.'],['1858','Transfer of rule to the British Crown.']], 'A common enemy does not automatically mean identical objectives among all rebels.', 'How can a short answer acknowledge both unity and diversity in 1857?', 'Identify common opposition and shared symbols, then illustrate distinct military, dynastic and local aims. Finish by explaining uneven regional participation and why neither total unity nor complete isolation describes the evidence.'),
  reform:D('Reform, education and the public sphere',[
    ['Compare approaches, not only founders','Movements differed in how they approached scripture, caste, women’s position and social authority. Some sought reinterpretation within a tradition; others challenged hierarchy more directly. An organisation’s stated ideals should be distinguished from its membership and actual reach. A comparison becomes useful when it asks whose problems were prioritised and which methods were available.'],
    ['Education and unequal access','Debates about language and curriculum were also debates about employment, culture and power. The 1835 decision and Wood’s Despatch of 1854 belong to different policy moments. Institutions widened learning for some while leaving major inequalities. Women and marginalised communities developed their own educational initiatives and arguments, so reform should not be narrated only as elite men acting on others.'],
    ['Print as infrastructure for debate','Newspapers and pamphlets helped circulate grievances, proposals and new identities. Editors mattered, but language, literacy, price and distribution shaped readership. Public readings could carry printed ideas beyond individual subscribers. Censorship and prosecution reveal official concern about this circulation. Link a publication to a debate rather than learning its title without historical purpose.']
  ],[['Brahmo Samaj','Religious and social reform','Analyse reinterpretation and social constituency.'],['Arya Samaj','Reform through an appeal to Vedic authority','Compare its approach with other reform traditions.'],['Aligarh movement','Education and community advancement','Situate institutions within colonial conditions.'],['Anti-caste initiatives','Challenges to inherited hierarchy','Do not subsume all reform under elite religious organisations.']], [['Public sphere','Spaces and media through which public questions are debated.'],['Social reform','Organised attempts to change practices or institutions.'],['Censorship','Control over publication or circulation of expression.']], [['1828','Brahmo Sabha founded.'],['1835','Major colonial education-policy decision.'],['1854','Wood’s Despatch.'],['1878','Vernacular Press Act.']], 'English education did not reach everyone, and educated Indians did not hold one political opinion.', 'What would make a press-history answer more than a list?', 'Explain audiences, language, circulation, censorship and the debate a paper advanced. Show how print connected people and grievances while recognising unequal access.'),
  nationalism:D('Early nationalism, Swadeshi and competing strategies',[
    ['Making a wider political platform','Associations and newspapers helped connect regional grievances with questions about colonial rule. The Congress offered a forum for demands, discussion and political education. Early leaders used constitutional methods and developed an economic critique. Their limited social base is important, but it should not obscure institution building or the creation of arguments later movements could use.'],
    ['Swadeshi as practical politics','Opposition to Bengal’s partition connected boycott with indigenous enterprise, national education and cultural expression. These activities brought politics into consumption and everyday institutions. Participation remained uneven, and sustaining alternatives required resources. The movement’s significance lies partly in its methods of mobilisation, not only in whether every immediate aim succeeded.'],
    ['Different routes to freedom','Revolutionary groups, overseas networks and Home Rule campaigns differed in organisation and strategy. Wartime conditions intensified political expectations. The Lucknow Pact created an agreement in a particular context; it did not permanently resolve every disagreement. Place constitutional negotiation, mass activity and revolutionary action alongside one another instead of treating any one strand as the entire movement.']
  ],[['Moderate methods','Petitions, public argument and reform demands','Assess political education as well as limits.'],['Swadeshi','Boycott and indigenous alternatives','Connect economic practice with mobilisation.'],['Home Rule','Public campaigns for self-government','Different from clandestine armed activity.'],['Ghadar','Revolutionary overseas networks','Connect migration, war and anti-colonial organisation.']], [['Boycott','Organised refusal to use or participate in something.'],['Swadeshi','Support for indigenous production and institutions.'],['Constitutional agitation','Political action through demands concerning lawful institutions and rights.']], [['1885','Indian National Congress founded.'],['1905','Bengal partition and Swadeshi mobilisation.'],['1907','Surat split.'],['1916','Home Rule activity and Lucknow Pact.']], 'The labels moderate and extremist describe political tendencies in a specific context, not fixed personality types.', 'Why did Swadeshi require constructive activity as well as boycott?', 'Refusing imported goods or existing institutions created a need for alternatives. Schools, production and organisations helped turn protest into sustained participation, although resources and social reach limited success.'),
  gandhi:D('Gandhian mobilisation: methods, participation and limits',[
    ['A local grievance can become political education','Champaran, Ahmedabad and Kheda addressed different problems. Inquiry, negotiation and local organisation connected grievance with disciplined resistance. These campaigns should not be compressed into a single identical event. They helped establish a political method and networks, while depending on participants who already had knowledge of local conditions and their own demands.'],
    ['Mass action is interpreted locally','A national programme can acquire different meanings among peasants, workers, students and merchants. Leaders seek coordination, but participants bring their own priorities. Non-Cooperation emphasised withdrawal of support; Civil Disobedience included deliberate breaches of selected laws. Compare programme, participation and actual action rather than assuming that instructions were followed uniformly.'],
    ['Withdrawal, negotiation and debate','Chauri Chaura exposed the tension between rapid mobilisation and non-violent discipline. The Gandhi–Irwin Pact reflects another tension: negotiation could create opportunities while disappointing some participants. Gandhi’s educational and national ideas also differed from Tagore’s without making them absolute opposites. Analyse choices in context, weighing achievements, limits and alternative perspectives.']
  ],[['Champaran, 1917','Indigo cultivators','Local inquiry and agrarian grievances.'],['Ahmedabad, 1918','Textile labour dispute','Industrial workers rather than a revenue campaign.'],['Non-Cooperation','Withdrawal from colonial links','Compare programme with local interpretations.'],['Civil Disobedience','Violation of selected laws','Salt connected a national demand to an everyday issue.']], [['Satyagraha','Resistance grounded in truth, ethical discipline and non-violence.'],['Constructive work','Practical activity intended to build social and political capacity.'],['Mass mobilisation','Bringing large and diverse groups into collective action.']], [['1917–18','Early local campaigns.'],['1920–22','Non-Cooperation.'],['1929','Complete independence adopted as the Congress objective.'],['1930–31','Salt March, Civil Disobedience and Gandhi–Irwin Pact.']], 'A movement’s withdrawal does not prove that nobody supported it or that every achievement vanished.', 'Compare the political use of salt with an abstract constitutional demand.', 'Salt taxation touched ordinary life, made defiance understandable and enabled visible participation. Its political force came from connecting a familiar grievance with the larger claim to self-rule.'),
  constitution:D('Constitutional change and the limits of colonial reform',[
    ['Read laws through institutions','Constitutional history becomes manageable when each measure is linked to an institutional question: who controlled the executive, who could represent whom, and which powers remained reserved? A title change is not equivalent to democratic accountability. The Governor-General’s evolving designation, parliamentary supervision and later representative institutions belong to different parts of this development.'],
    ['Representation and safeguards','Separate electorates, reserved seats and joint electorates are distinct arrangements. The Communal Award and Poona Pact should be compared on precisely this point. The debate involved competing concerns about political voice and social power. Avoid describing the agreement as merely an increase or decrease in seats without explaining how representatives would be chosen.'],
    ['Proposal versus implementation','The 1935 Act introduced provincial autonomy but its proposed all-India federation did not operate. Congress ministries formed after the 1937 elections within a constrained colonial framework. Governors’ powers and central control limited their autonomy. Use these limits to explain the difference between running a provincial ministry and possessing national sovereignty.']
  ],[['1773 framework','Governor-General of Bengal','An early stage of parliamentary regulation.'],['1833 Act','Governor-General of India','Administrative centralisation, not popular sovereignty.'],['1919 Act','Provincial dyarchy','Different fields of government assigned differently.'],['1935 Act','Provincial autonomy; federation proposed','Only implemented provisions should be described as operating institutions.']], [['Dyarchy','Division of governmental subjects between different authorities.'],['Separate electorate','An electoral arrangement in which a defined group votes separately for its representatives.'],['Reserved seat','A seat set aside for a group, which can exist within joint electorates.']], [['1909','Constitutional reform and separate electorates.'],['1919','Provincial dyarchy.'],['1932','Communal Award and Poona Pact.'],['1935–37','Act followed by provincial elections and ministries.']], 'The all-India federation proposed in 1935 never came into operation.', 'Why is the distinction between joint electorates and reserved seats important?', 'A joint electorate concerns who votes, while reservation concerns which seats or representatives are set aside. The two can coexist, so neither term should be used as a synonym for the other.'),
  independence:D('War, Quit India and the transfer of power',[
    ['Wartime pressures','The Second World War changed economic conditions and political bargaining. Congress ministries resigned after India was drawn into the war without consultation. The failure of later proposals, including the Cripps Mission, helped create the setting for Quit India. Inflation, uncertainty and the Japanese advance were part of the context; no single factor is a complete explanation.'],
    ['Resistance under repression','The arrests of national leaders were followed by diverse local actions and underground organisation. Bihar’s underground networks, including Jayaprakash Narayan’s role, connect regional participation to the wider movement. The INA’s trials and the naval uprising later added different pressures. Their importance should be assessed alongside mass politics and Britain’s postwar situation, not as exclusive causes of independence.'],
    ['Negotiation and human consequences','The Cabinet Mission proposed a framework for a united India, while later decisions accepted partition. Disagreements, political competition and violence shaped this transition. Independence transferred authority but also involved institutional division and enormous displacement. An answer on Partition should bring constitutional negotiations into conversation with the experiences of people affected, without treating violence as a minor afterthought.']
  ],[['Cripps Mission','Wartime constitutional proposal','Failure belongs to the immediate setting of Quit India.'],['Quit India','Mass and underground resistance','Repression and local initiative shaped its course.'],['INA trials','Political impact beyond military outcomes','Public responses matter as well as battlefield results.'],['Cabinet Mission','Proposed united constitutional framework','Not the same as the later Partition settlement.']], [['Interim government','A temporary executive during a political transition.'],['Sovereignty','Supreme authority to govern.'],['Partition','Division of territory and institutions, accompanied here by mass displacement and violence.']], [['1939','War and resignation of Congress ministries.'],['1942','Cripps Mission and Quit India.'],['1946','Cabinet Mission and interim government.'],['1947','Independence and Partition.']], 'Quit India did not immediately end British rule; its importance includes political effects beyond immediate success.', 'How can a balanced answer explain independence without a single decisive cause?', 'Combine sustained popular mobilisation, constitutional negotiation, wartime and postwar pressures, changing legitimacy and unrest. Explain how they interacted, then distinguish the achievement of independence from the separate question of why Partition occurred.')
};

